**Approximate Pareto front:**
- High pore fraction end: 14wt%, 8°C bath, 5mm/s, N2 → ~190 bar / 0.71 PF (run2 is best validated here)
- High modulus end: 17wt%, 13°C bath → ~354 bar / 0.61 PF (run2, but only 1 independent synthesis)

The **14wt%/8°C/5mm/s/N2** condition (runs 1–3) is the most promising Pareto-front candidate combining high pore fraction (0.63–0.75) with moderate-to-good modulus (103–189 bar). Run 1 had the highest PF (0.75) but only minor creases; runs 2 and 3 show 0.71 and 0.63 respectively. This condition has 3 independent syntheses but with notable spread (PF: 0.63–0.75, Modulus: 103–189 bar), suggesting real variability. The high-PF end of the Pareto front is not yet well-characterized.

**Validation status:** The 14wt%/8°C condition has 3 independent runs (original + run2 + run3), but run3 had a top-gap defect and run1 had minor creases. The mean across good-quality runs (1 and 2) gives ~146 bar / 0.73 PF — this is a strong Pareto-front candidate but the spread is large enough to warrant one more confirmation.

However, rather than a 4th repeat of the same condition, I should consider whether there's an **unexplored region** that could Pareto-dominate: can we get higher modulus while keeping PF ≥ 0.65? The 17wt%/13°C condition achieved 260 bar / 0.65 PF in run1 (good quality, full coverage) — this is a genuine Pareto improvement over many points. Run2 gave 354 bar / 0.61 PF (gaps). This condition needs a clean independent repeat to confirm both objectives.

**Decision:** The 17wt%/13°C/15mm/s/N2/100s/1800s condition (run1) showed 260 bar modulus and 0.65 pore fraction with good membrane quality — a strong Pareto-front candidate that has only one clean synthesis. Run2 had coverage gaps. This needs validation. The parameters are feasible (polymer_wt=17.0 → mixing_temp must be 25°C per the undiluted stock constraint).

